; SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
; SPDX-FileCopyrightText: The ClamFleet Authors
;
; SELinux module for the ClamFleet agent on the RHEL 9 and 10 family.
; Install and remove it at priority 300 ONLY: `semodule -X 300 -i clamfleet.cil` and
; `semodule -X 300 -r clamfleet`. A plain `semodule -r` removes at the default priority
; and would leave this module loaded.
;
; What it is for. clamd runs the VirusEvent command (`clamfleet-agent event`) in its own
; domain, antivirus_t, and that command must write one file into the spool. Under the
; targeted policy it cannot: the spool is var_lib_t, which antivirus_t may not write.
; Without this module every detection is dropped on the floor (clamd's journal shows
; "event not spooled ... permission denied") and nothing alerts.
;
; What it gives, and to whom. antivirus_t is the domain of clamd, freshclam AND clamonacc
; alike, so every rule below is granted to all three. The rules are therefore written
; ONLY on our own type, clamfleet_spool_t, which labels nothing but the event spool and
; the spool's accounting file:
;   /var/lib/clamfleet/spool/events(/.*)?   where clamd's user writes one file per detection
;   /var/lib/clamfleet/spool/usage          the accounting file `event` updates under a lock
; freshclam and clamonacc may therefore also write there. They run as clamd's own group,
; which the milestone 5 trust boundary already trusts to originate events (docs/detections.md).
; The agent's other directories (spool/reports, spool/quarantine) and the token stay
; var_lib_t / etc_t and are out of reach of antivirus_t: tested by TestSELinuxEventCannotWriteElsewhere.
;
; What it does NOT need, proven on AlmaLinux 9.8 (policy of that release) with the AVC log:
;   - no exec rule: antivirus_t may already execute shell_exec_t and bin_t files without a
;     transition (`sesearch -A -s antivirus_t -t bin_t -c file`), and clamd starts
;     /bin/sh -c "<VirusEvent>" that way;
;   - no rule for var_lib_t (searching /var/lib/clamfleet and spool/) and none for etc_t
;     (reading /etc/clamfleet/agent.yaml): the stock policy already grants both.
; So NO rule in this module is written on a stock type. TestSELinuxRuleSet fails when the
; module adds anything else to `sesearch -A -s antivirus_t`.
;
; The agent service (`run`, unconfined_service_t) reads and deletes in clamfleet_spool_t
; with no extra rules: unconfined domains may access every file type.
;
; Known stock denial, unrelated to us and not fixed here: clamd reads /selinux/enforce.

(type clamfleet_spool_t)
(roletype object_r clamfleet_spool_t)
; file_type is what makes this a labelable file type (and what unconfined access is granted on).
(typeattributeset file_type (clamfleet_spool_t))

(filecon "/var/lib/clamfleet/spool/events(/.*)?" any (system_u object_r clamfleet_spool_t ((s0) (s0))))
(filecon "/var/lib/clamfleet/spool/usage" file (system_u object_r clamfleet_spool_t ((s0) (s0))))

; The two rules sit in an optional block because antivirus_t is not always in the policy. EL10 moved
; the antivirus module out of the base targeted policy into selinux-policy-targeted-extra (CRB), so on a
; stock EL10 the type does not exist, CIL cannot resolve the allow statements, and a module without the
; block is rejected whole (R1 decision 9). CIL disables an optional block whose names do not resolve and
; evaluates it again on every policy rebuild: the rules switch on by themselves when the extra package is
; installed and off when it is removed (or on EL9 after `semodule -d antivirus`). Without the block that
; removal would make every later policy rebuild fail. The type, roletype, file_type and filecon stay
; OUTSIDE the block on purpose: they name only our own type and `file_type`, which both releases have,
; and the label must exist whether or not clamd is confined. deploy/test/selinux_test.sh accepts exactly
; one optional block holding exactly these two rules.
(optional clamfleet_antivirus
	; Directory: look up and stat the entries, open and fsync the directory, create and remove
	; the temporary file and the final hard link. `read` is needed to open the directory for fsync.
	(allow antivirus_t clamfleet_spool_t (dir (search getattr open read write add_name remove_name)))
	; Files: create the temp file, write it, chmod it (setattr), hard-link it to its final name,
	; remove the temp name, and read, write and flock the shared accounting file (lock).
	(allow antivirus_t clamfleet_spool_t (file (create open read write getattr setattr lock link unlink)))
)
